inflation is crushing pakistan and we  just got news that the imf is now going  to step in the imf has officially  extended a brand new loan to the country  of pakistan their idea is that they want  to avert a potential default and they  want to pave the way for more aid  according to bloomberg this imf loan is  important because pakistan needs at  least 41 billion dollars in the next 12  months to repay debt and fund imports as  foreign exchange reserves shrink to a  level that could only cover less than  two months of their imports now of  course whenever you see money sloshing  around the next question should be  what's their inflation rate and is it  getting better or is it getting worse  steve hank one of the economists that  people pay attention to in some of these  emerging markets he pointed out that his  current calculation of pakistan  inflation is at a stunning 43 percent  year-over-year he said that recently the  imf agreed to release 1.17 billion  dollars in funds for pakistan but he  doesn't believe that another imf loan  will save pakistan's economy like all  the past imf loans this one will fail as  well  now as we take a look at the global  standings of inflation we can see that  pakistan sitting with about 40 percent  or more inflation they're doing better  than places like ukraine leos venezuela  argentina cuba zimbabwe but sitting with  more than 40 percent increase in prices  year over year isn't exactly reassuring  to the people on the ground now when we  look at these local economies what we  have to remember is that the growth of  the money supply ends up creating issues  but it's not just the government  themselves the more debt that they take  on as they try to inject this capital  into every corner of their economy it  ends up driving more and more money  chasing fewer and fewer goods the  country of pakistan with more than 40  inflation is already in trouble but they  have so much debt that they can't pay it  back and so now here comes the  international monetary fund they're  going to step in and they're going to  give them more debt in order to be able  to pay off the old debt that's like an  individual consumer saying i have my  first credit card and i can't pay it off  so let me take out a second credit card  to pay off the first one and then when i  can't pay back my second credit card  i'll take out a third credit card to pay  off the second more and more debt is not  the solution ultimately we need to  return to some sort of sound money  digital sound money where it is not  based on countries taking on more and  more debt  punishing the value of the money that is  held by their everyday citizens we need  a world where people can save till  financial security and we need a world  where it's not based on how much debt  can you take  and how can you go and beg people like  the imf for more money ultimately the  citizens of pakistan they want to live a  happy life they want to simply pursue  financial security for them and their  families  and what we have to do is start to  implement first principle solutions to  these problems or we simply kick the can  down the road create more volatility  create more problems and ultimately  relearn the lesson decade after decade  after decade market intervention will  never be as sustainable of a solution as  allowing the free market to solve our  problems
 

